1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,186
Your SSD has no moving parts.

2
00:00:02,206 --> 00:00:04,221
So where do your files actually live?

3
00:00:04,493 --> 00:00:05,820
Inside billions of tiny

4
00:00:05,840 --> 00:00:07,228
traps for electrons.

5
00:00:07,581 --> 00:00:08,653
Let's open one up and follow a

6
00:00:08,673 --> 00:00:10,200
single photo as it gets saved.

7
00:00:10,658 --> 00:00:12,812
Every flash chip is a grid of cells.

8
00:00:13,166 --> 00:00:14,353
Think of each cell as a tiny

9
00:00:14,373 --> 00:00:15,471
bucket with a gate.

10
00:00:16,045 --> 00:00:16,966
Push electrons in,

11
00:00:16,986 --> 00:00:18,069
and they stay trapped,

12
00:00:18,089 --> 00:00:19,360
even with the power off.

13
00:00:19,935 --> 00:00:21,099
A full bucket and an empty

14
00:00:21,119 --> 00:00:22,600
bucket mean one and zero.

15
00:00:22,953 --> 00:00:24,643
But modern drives are greedy.

16
00:00:24,997 --> 00:00:25,894
Fill the bucket to four

17
00:00:25,914 --> 00:00:26,532
different levels,

18
00:00:26,552 --> 00:00:28,300
and one cell holds two bits.

19
00:00:28,653 --> 00:00:30,215
Eight levels, three bits.

20
00:00:30,569 --> 00:00:31,443
Sixteen levels,

21
00:00:31,463 --> 00:00:32,317
four bits.

22
00:00:32,671 --> 00:00:34,183
That's QLC, and it's why big

23
00:00:34,203 --> 00:00:35,393
drives got so cheap.

24
00:00:35,852 --> 00:00:37,866
Then engineers stopped building flat.

25
00:00:38,220 --> 00:00:40,011
They stack the cells into towers,

26
00:00:40,031 --> 00:00:41,509
over 300 layers high,

27
00:00:41,529 --> 00:00:42,414
on a chip smaller than

28
00:00:42,434 --> 00:00:43,369
your fingernail.

29
00:00:43,944 --> 00:00:45,328
Your photo reaches the controller,

30
00:00:45,348 --> 00:00:47,247
the drive's own tiny processor.

31
00:00:47,601 --> 00:00:49,287
It splits the photo into pages,

32
00:00:49,307 --> 00:00:50,100
spreads them across

33
00:00:50,120 --> 00:00:51,261
every chip at once,

34
00:00:51,281 --> 00:00:52,739
and writes them in parallel.

35
00:00:53,197 --> 00:00:54,826
That's how a fast drive reads over

36
00:00:54,846 --> 00:00:56,721
7,000 megabytes per second.

37
00:00:56,993 --> 00:00:57,998
Here's the catch.

38
00:00:58,352 --> 00:00:59,237
Every time a bucket is

39
00:00:59,257 --> 00:01:00,410
emptied and refilled,

40
00:01:00,430 --> 00:01:01,632
its gate wears a little.

41
00:01:01,986 --> 00:01:03,603
After one to three thousand rewrites,

42
00:01:03,623 --> 00:01:04,697
it starts to leak.

43
00:01:04,726 --> 00:01:06,241
So the controller cheats.

44
00:01:06,514 --> 00:01:08,238
It never rewrites the same cell.

45
00:01:08,325 --> 00:01:09,304
Every new write goes to

46
00:01:09,324 --> 00:01:10,349
the least worn cell,

47
00:01:10,369 --> 00:01:11,324
and old data gets moved

48
00:01:11,344 --> 00:01:12,604
quietly in the background.

49
00:01:12,632 --> 00:01:13,599
That's why a typical one

50
00:01:13,619 --> 00:01:15,062
terabyte drive is rated for

51
00:01:15,082 --> 00:01:16,490
600 terabytes written.

52
00:01:16,510 --> 00:01:18,092
That's over 300 gigabytes every

53
00:01:18,112 --> 00:01:19,497
single day for five years.

54
00:01:19,517 --> 00:01:20,588
So your photo isn't

55
00:01:20,608 --> 00:01:21,738
stored in one place.

56
00:01:21,758 --> 00:01:23,212
It's charge, spread across

57
00:01:23,232 --> 00:01:24,605
towers of tiny buckets,

58
00:01:24,625 --> 00:01:25,511
constantly shuffled to

59
00:01:25,531 --> 00:01:26,698
keep your drive alive.

60
00:01:26,727 --> 00:01:28,207
Follow for part two:

61
00:01:28,236 --> 00:01:29,972
what happens when you hit delete.
